"Surah Yusuf begins with the letters Alif, Lam, Ra. These letters are the verses of the clear book. The word 'mubin' means that which is clear and distinct in its own essence, while at the same time clarifying to others. In this respect, the Quran is a book that clarifies everything.\n\nThere are four aspects to the Quran being 'mubin'. The first is that it is a miracle; no human or jinn has been able to produce the like of it. The second is that it explains the fundamentals and principles of religion, rulings, news of the unseen, and stories. The third is that it distinguishes truth from falsehood. The fourth is the beauty of its language and expression; it was revealed in the most eloquent dialect of Arabic, leaving men of letters powerless before its composition.\n\nThis surah, which comes after Surahs Yunus and Hud, emphasizes the clarificatory quality of the Quran. Allah states that He revealed the Quran in Arabic. This means that not only its meaning, but also its wording is in Arabic, so that people of understanding may comprehend it.\n\nThis surah narrates the best of stories: the story of Joseph (Yusuf). Allah informed our Prophet of this story through revelation; prior to this, he had no knowledge of it. This is proof that it came from Allah. The expression 'ahsan al-qasas' means both the best of stories and the finest manner of narration.\n\nJoseph saw in his dream eleven stars, the sun, and the moon prostrating to him. His father, Jacob (Ya'qub), told him not to recount this dream to his brothers, lest they plot against him out of jealousy. Furthermore, he gave glad tidings that Allah would choose Joseph, teach him the interpretation of dreams, and bless him and the family of Jacob with prophethood.\n\nHis brothers were jealous because they found Joseph and his full brother Benjamin more beloved to their father than themselves. First they planned to kill him, then to cast him into a well. One of them suggested that he be thrown into a well so that a caravan might find him and take him away. They asked their father for permission to let Joseph go with them to play.\n\nJacob warned them of his fear of a wolf, but his brothers insisted. In the end, they took Joseph away and cast him into the well. Allah revealed to Joseph that he would eventually inform them of what they had done. His brothers returned to their father weeping, claiming that a wolf had eaten Joseph, and showed the shirt stained with false blood. Jacob took refuge in Allah with beautiful patience.\n\nA caravan arrived and lowered their bucket into the well; they found Joseph, drew him out, and sold him for a few dirhams. An Egyptian minister (the Aziz) bought him and told his wife to take good care of him, thinking they might adopt him as a son. Allah established Joseph in Egypt and taught him the interpretation of dreams.\n\nWhen Joseph reached the age of maturity, Allah granted him judgment and knowledge. Thus are the doers of good rewarded. Then, the lady in whose house he resided (Zulaikha) sought to seduce him; she locked the doors and invited him to herself. Joseph refused, taking refuge in Allah and stating that his master had treated him well and that wrongdoers would never prosper. She had truly resolved upon it, and Joseph would have inclined toward her had he not seen the proof of his Lord. Allah turned indecency and evil away from him, for he was among the chosen servants endowed with sincerity. Here the concepts of 'mukhlas' and 'mukhlis' are explained; the former refers to one chosen by Allah, while the latter refers to a sincere servant.\n\nBoth raced to the door, and the woman tore his shirt from behind. When they encountered her husband at the door, the woman accused Joseph and demanded punishment. Joseph, however, said that it was she who had sought to seduce him. A member of the woman's household bore witness that if the shirt was torn from the front, the woman was truthful, but if it was torn from the back, Joseph was truthful. When it was seen that the shirt was torn from the back, the husband said that this was the plot of women; he told Joseph not to mention this matter and commanded the woman to seek forgiveness for her sin.\n\nWhen the women in the city spread gossip, the wife of the Aziz invited them, prepared a lavish banquet, gave each of them a knife, and had Joseph come out before them. When the women saw him, they cut their hands and exclaimed, 'This is an angel!' The woman said, 'This is he about whom you blamed me; I sought to seduce him, but he remained chaste. If he does not do what I command him, he will be thrown into prison and will be among the disgraced.' Joseph prayed to Allah, stating that prison was dearer to him than that to which the women invited him, and asked to be protected from their snare. Allah answered his prayer. Nonetheless, the thought of imprisoning Joseph for a time prevailed.\n\nTwo young men also entered the prison. One of them dreamed that he was pressing wine, while the other dreamed that he was carrying bread on his head from which birds were eating. Joseph told them that he would inform them of the interpretation before their food arrived. First, he called them to believe in Allah and to abandon the religion of a people who deny the Hereafter. Then, he informed them that one would serve wine to his master, while the other would be crucified and birds would eat from his head. To the one he believed would be saved, he said, 'Mention me to your master.' However, Satan caused him to forget this, and Joseph remained in prison for years. Some commentators have noted that Joseph should have asked of Allah rather than relying on a mortal servant.\n\nOne day, the king recounted his dream in which seven lean cows were devouring seven fat cows, and he saw seven green ears of grain and seven dry ones. The notables said, 'These are confused dreams; we do not know their interpretation.' Here, Elmalılı explains at length the difference between ru'ya (a true dream) and hulum (a confused dream). While ru'ya carries a genuine and interpretable meaning, hulum consists merely of meaningless delusions. A dream can be a divine inspiration coming from Allah or brought by an angel. Islamic scholars divide dreams into three categories: divine inspiration, psychological imaginations, and satanic suggestions. In a hadith of the Prophet (peace be upon him), a dream is mentioned as one of the forty-six parts of prophethood.\n\nElmalılı Hamdi Yazır dwells upon the nature of dreams. According to him, a dream is an unveiling (kashfi) phenomenon, distinct from the mere association of ordinary memories. Whether in sleep or while awake, they are truths reflected onto the soul from the world of forms (alam al-mithal). The interpretation of dreams, on the other hand, is a scholarly, divinely bestowed, and intuitive knowledge; it cannot be resolved by formal logic. For this reason, distinguishing a true dream from ahlam (confused dreams) is difficult. In fact, those who are heedless of the secrets of the unseen consider all dreams to be ahlam. Pharaoh's counselors, too, in their ignorance, had said, 'We do not know the interpretation of ahlam.'\n\nThe cupbearer who was released from prison remembered Joseph after a long time and went to the prison to learn the interpretation of the king's dream. Joseph interpreted the dream of seven lean cows devouring seven fat cows and seven green ears of grain and seven dry ones: they would cultivate abundantly for seven years and leave what they reaped in its ear; then seven years of drought would follow, consuming what they had stored; thereafter would come a rainy year in which the people would find relief from distress.\n\nWhen the king heard this interpretation, he ordered that Joseph be brought to him. However, when the messenger arrived, Joseph asked him to return to his master before he left prison and inquire what the intention of the women who cut their hands was. The king questioned the women; they all stated that they knew no evil of Joseph. The wife of the Aziz confessed, saying, 'Now the truth has come to light; it was I who sought to seduce him, and he is indeed of the truthful.' The woman added that she did not betray Joseph behind his back and did not absolve her own soul, for the soul surely enjoins evil, except for those upon whom her Lord has mercy.\n\nThe ruler summoned Joseph (Yusuf), wishing to make him his special advisor. When he spoke with him, he said, \"Today, you are in our presence established in position and trusted.\" Joseph requested a post, saying, \"Place me over the storehouses of this land; for I am indeed a skilled keeper and knowledgeable.\" Elmalılı notes here that the \"Aziz\" and the \"King (Malik)\" were different individuals; that the King was a ruler worthy of praise and did not use the title of Pharaoh.\n\nAllah established Joseph in Egypt; he settled wherever he wished within it. This was a manifestation of divine mercy. The reward of those who do good is not lost, and the reward of the Hereafter is even better. During the years of famine, Joseph's brothers came to Egypt. Joseph recognized them, but they did not recognize him. While having their provisions prepared, he said, \"Bring me your other brother from your father; if you do not bring him to me, there will be no grain for you.\" The brothers said that they would try to obtain permission from their father. Joseph commanded his servants to put his brothers' capital into their saddlebags so that they might return again.\n\nUpon their first arrival in Egypt, Joseph recognized his brothers, yet they did not recognize him. Joseph asked them about their family, and they stated that in addition to their father Jacob (Ya'qub), they had another brother in place of the one who had died. Thereupon, Joseph meant Benjamin (Binyamin) when he said, \"Bring me your other brother from your father.\" He treated them generously, saying, \"I give full measure and I am the best of hosts,\" but he told them that if they did not bring their brother, it would not be possible for them to obtain grain again. In response to this persistent demand, the brothers stated that they would try to persuade their father.\n\nAs Joseph saw his brothers off, he instructed his servants to secretly place the price they had paid inside their saddlebags. He did this so that they would rejoice upon their return and be eager to come back. This precaution was to assist them in persuading their father to bring Benjamin.\n\nWhen the brothers returned to their father Jacob, they said, \"O our father! Measure has been denied to us, so send our brother with us that we may obtain grain. We will certainly guard him.\" Jacob expressed his distrust of them, saying, \"Can I entrust him to you except as I entrusted his brother before?\" However, when they opened their baggage and saw that their capital had been returned to them, they tried to persuade their father by explaining this situation to him.\n\nJacob consented to send Benjamin only after taking a firm oath from them. He said to his sons, \"Give me an oath by Allah that you will bring him back to me unless you are completely overwhelmed.\" They gave their oath. In addition, he advised them to enter through separate gates upon entering Egypt; the reason for this was to avoid drawing attention and being envied as a group. However, he also emphasized that this precaution could not avert the decree of Allah.\n\nWhen the brothers arrived in Egypt, they entered before Joseph. Joseph took Benjamin to himself, introduced himself, and said, \"I am your brother, so do not grieve over what they have been doing.\" According to a narration, Joseph told him what his brothers had done to him in the past and instructed him not to be deceived this time. Then, formulating a plan, he had the drinking cup placed into Benjamin's saddlebag.\n\nAfter the caravan set out, a crier called out: \"O caravan! You are indeed thieves!\" The brothers turned back and asked, \"What are you looking for?\" The officials replied, \"We are looking for the King's drinking cup, and whoever brings it shall have a camel-load of grain.\" The brothers rejected the accusation, saying, \"By Allah, we are not thieves, nor did we come to cause corruption.\" When the officials asked, \"What shall be the penalty if you are found to be liars?\", they answered, \"The penalty is that he in whose bag it is found shall himself be the penalty.\"\n\nA search was conducted, and the drinking cup was brought out from Benjamin's bag. In this way, Joseph retained his brother with him. The brothers made an insinuation against Joseph, saying, \"If he stole, a brother of his stole before him.\" Joseph said within himself, \"You are in a far worse position, and Allah knows best what you assert.\" Thereupon, the brothers pleaded with Joseph to consider their elderly father and retain one of them in his place, but Joseph did not accept.\n\nThis action of Joseph was intended to lead his brothers to regret their past mistakes and to repent. Moreover, it was to help them fulfill the oath they had made to their father. All these measures took place with the revelation and permission of Allah. No precaution can avert the decree of Allah; Jacob's advice in this regard also demonstrates the balance between taking precautions (tadbir) and trusting in Allah (tawakkul).\n\nWhen asked what the punishment would be for the person who stole Joseph's drinking cup, the brothers stated that the thief himself would be seized and taken as property. This was the punishment for theft in the religious law (sharia) of the Prophet Jacob: the thief would serve the owner of the stolen property for a period of one year. By prompting them to issue this legal verdict (fatwa), Joseph secured the right to retain his brother Benjamin.\n\nJoseph began the search with the bags of the other brothers first, and then took out the drinking cup from Benjamin's bag. There is an important point here: the object that emerged from the bag was not the King's drinking cup, but Joseph's own bowl. If it had been the King's cup, Joseph would have had to act according to Egyptian law and would not have been able to retain Benjamin. However, by placing his own bowl, he detained him based on the legal verdict his brothers had given. In this way, he neither violated Egyptian law nor failed to let them be judged according to their own religion.\n\nFaced with this situation, the brothers, feeling helpless, angrily said, \"If he stole, a brother of his committed theft before him.\" According to one narration, Joseph had broken his grandfather's idol as a child. Joseph concealed this remark within himself, did not reveal it to them, and was patient. Then the brothers pleaded with Joseph, imploring for mercy by addressing him, \"O Aziz!\"\n\nWhen the brothers lost hope, their eldest, Judah (Yahuda), decided to stay in Egypt and told them to return to their father. Jacob did not believe the news they brought and said, \"No, your souls have enticed you to something. So patience is most fitting for me. Perhaps Allah will bring them all back to me.\" Then he turned away, saying, \"O grief for Joseph, this is too much!\" and his eyes turned white from sorrow. Although his sons attempted to console him, Jacob stated that he complained of his grief and sorrow only to Allah, and he sent them to search for Joseph and his brother.\n\nJacob encouraged them, saying, \"Do not despair of Allah's mercy; for no one despairs of Allah's mercy except the disbelieving people.\" Thereupon, the brothers went to Egypt once again and entered Joseph's presence. Joseph revealed his identity to them, saying, \"I am Joseph, and this is my brother. Allah has indeed favored us. Whoever fears Allah and remains patient, Allah never allows the reward of those who do good to be lost.\" When the brothers acknowledged their mistake, Joseph forgave them and told them to take his shirt to their father.\n\nWhen they met Joseph for the third time, a great softening and awakening had taken place in the brothers' hearts. This softening, which had begun with Benjamin's detention, progressively deepened and matured them. Joseph's forbearance toward them, his humility, and his sincerity also influenced them. For this reason, Joseph realized that the time had come to reveal his identity, and he asked them, \"Do you know what you did with Joseph and his brother when you were ignorant?\" With this question, what had been promised earlier when he was cast into the well was fulfilled.\n\nJoseph's words struck their hearts like lightning, and they immediately realized that he was Joseph. In great astonishment and excitement, they said, \"Are you really Joseph?\" Joseph identified himself, saying, \"I am Joseph, and this is my brother,\" and giving thanks to Allah, he said: \"Indeed, Allah has favored us. Truly, whoever fears Allah and is patient, Allah does not allow the reward of the doers of good to be lost.\" Thereupon, his brothers confessed, saying, \"By Allah, Allah has indeed preferred you over us. We were truly in the wrong.\"\n\nJoseph said to his brothers, \"There is no reproach against you today.\" The word 'tathrib' (tathrib) means censuring the wrongdoer and calling them to account. Accepting their apology, Joseph stated that he hoped Allah would also forgive them. He then gave them his shirt and said, \"Take this shirt of mine and cast it over my father's face so that his sight may return.\" It is noted that this shirt was a counterpart to the bloodstained shirt brought earlier. It is narrated that the shirt was a miraculous heirloom inherited from Abraham (peace be upon him).\n\nJoseph tasked his brothers with conveying the glad tidings to his father and bringing his entire family to Egypt. When the caravan departed from Egypt, Jacob said, \"I sense the scent of Joseph.\" Those around him accused him of senility. However, when the bearer of glad tidings arrived and placed the shirt over Jacob's face, his sight returned. Jacob said, \"Did I not tell you that I know from Allah that which you do not know?\" This event is regarded as a miracle, and although attempts are made to explain it with concepts such as telepathy, it is emphasized that it was a divine intervention.\n\nHis sons came and said, \"O our father, ask forgiveness for us from Allah.\" Jacob, however, postponed the prayer to a specific time, saying, \"I will seek forgiveness for you from my Lord later.\" It is noted that this was to wait until Joseph's forgiveness was ascertained. Finally, when the family arrived in Egypt and came before Joseph, Joseph embraced his parents and seated them upon a high throne. They all fell down in prostration before Joseph. Joseph expressed that the dream he had seen earlier had come true, saying, \"This is the fulfillment of my dream.\"\n\nGiving thanks to Allah, Joseph prayed: \"O my Lord! You have granted me a share of worldly dominion and taught me the interpretation of dreams. Take my soul as a Muslim, and join me with the righteous.\"\n\nJoseph took his mother and father up to his high mansion. Together with his brothers, they prostrated in gratitude to Allah. This prostration was not like the one in Joseph's dream, but a prostration of gratitude to Allah. Thus, the dream was fulfilled.\n\nTurning to his father, Joseph said, \"This is the interpretation of the dream I saw earlier. My Lord rescued me from prison and brought you here from the desert. Satan had sown discord between me and my brothers, but by the grace of Allah we have reached this fine end.\" In order not to embarrass his brothers, he made no mention of the incident of the well.\n\nJoseph prayed to Allah as follows: \"My Lord! You have given me dominion and knowledge. You are the Creator of the heavens and the earth. Take my soul as a Muslim and join me with the righteous.\" When he made this supplication, his soul was taken. His body was placed in a marble coffin and buried in the Nile River. Later, the rule changed in Egypt, and the Children of Israel became captives of the Pharaohs.\n\nAllah informed Muhammad of these accounts of the unseen through revelation. He was not with Joseph's brothers. However much you may desire it, most people will not believe. You ask no reward for this conveyance; this Quran is but a reminder for the worlds.\n\nHow many signs there are in the heavens and the earth that people encounter, yet they turn away from them! Most of them do not believe in Allah except while associating partners with Him. Or do they feel secure from Allah's punishment or the Hour that comes upon them suddenly? The way of the Prophet is to invite to Allah upon clear sight; he is not of the polytheists.\n\nThe messengers before you were also men from among the people of the cities, to whom revelation was given. Have they not traveled through the earth and seen the end of those who came before them? The home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful of Allah. When the messengers despaired, Our help came to them; those whom We willed were saved. Nor can Our punishment be averted from the criminal people.\n\nThere is certainly a lesson in their stories for people of deep understanding. This Quran is not an invented discourse; it confirms the scriptures before it and explains everything in detail. It is guidance and mercy for a people who believe. Following this surah comes Surah ar-Ra'd, which means 'Thunder'."